From f94c5ecce2866d2ef428fd73b06652eafe3e4a33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: axfor <aixiaoxiang2009@hotmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 21:27:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 26/40] lib/storage: collect a filtered TSDB status from the
 names of its series

With match[] set, getTSDBStatus finds the matching metricIDs and then scans
every row of the per-day inverted index to count them, so a status costs as
much as the day has series however few match. On a day of 241 million
series a selector of 30 thousand took 29-41 seconds, of which finding its
metricIDs took 0.1.

When the filters match at most -search.maxMetricIDsForDirectTSDBStatus
series, the status is now counted from their metric names instead, as
searchLabelNamesWithFiltersOnDate already does below 100 thousand. Pairs are
pushed into the top heaps in the order of the rows of the index, so that
ties at the edge of topN come out as the scan gives them. The count checks
the deadline as the scan does, leaves out the keys the index adds for its
own use, and gives way to the scan when the name of a matching series cannot
be found. The flag defaults to 0, which keeps the scan.

The two differ only where the scan miscounts: it takes a label whose name
has '=' in it for a value of the label its name begins with, and counts a
series listed twice in the per-day index twice.

The same statuses on that day now take 0.06-0.4 seconds, up to 4.4 the
first time after a restart, and return the same answer field by field. The
ACG ruler asks one for every large selector it cuts into shards; a first
day of three members caught up in 1271 seconds instead of 2133.
